|~ What is declared under `::verb`, the kind of a verb: the refusals
   its head raises, of its declaration, of the slots a call fills and of
   a contract called. ~|

:verb/VerbPayloadNotQuoteError
  |~~ `::verb` is a tag over a quote, and a payload of another kind is
      refused.

      ~(
        ::verb[1]
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbPayloadNotQuoteError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/VerbSlotWithoutBodyError
  |~~ The head of a verb is read before it runs, so a slot is declared
      by a kind, a set of kinds or a literal, and a leading declaration
      without a body is refused where the verb is made. A declaration
      whose body computes stands outside the head, the first step of the
      body.

      ~(
        ::verb~(:x |~~ How many. ~~| | add x)
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbSlotWithoutBodyError :x]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/VerbRoleNotKindError
  |~~ The roles of a verb, `:subject` and `:returns`, are declared by a
      kind or a set of kinds, and `:returns` by `/` for the subject's
      own kind.

      ~(
        ::verb~(:returns 3 | add 1)
        !| [type /role]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbRoleNotKindError :returns]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/VerbSlotMissingError
  |~~ A slot declared by a kind alone is filled by the call, and a call
      that leaves it empty is refused by the name of the slot.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | 5 | m
        !| [type /verbName /slot]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbSlotMissingError :m :x]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/VerbModifiersBeyondSlotsError
  |~~ A verb takes as many modifiers as it has slots, one more when
      the first is its subject, unless `*` gathers the ones that
      remain.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:x ::number | add x) | 5 | m 1 2 3
        !| [type /slotCount /actualCount]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbModifiersBeyondSlotsError 1 3]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/VerbSlotNotOfKindsError
  |~~ A slot declared by a set of kinds takes a value of one of them,
      reached through the tags the value carries, and refuses any
      other, since no one constructor reads it.

      ~(
        :m ::verb~(:limit #[::number ::null] | limit) | 5 | m "a"
        !| [type /slot /kinds]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbSlotNotOfKindsError :limit #[::null ::number]]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/VerbCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ A slot of code takes a quote `~(…)` or a verb and nothing else,
      since a value is never read as code, nor the error a modifier
      answered when it ran at the call.

      ~(
        :run ::verb~(:code ::quote | apply code) | 5 | run 1
        !| [type /slot]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbCodeNotQuoteError :code]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:verb/VerbWithoutBodyError
  |~~ The kind of the subject has no verb of the name: `:actualType`
      names the kind, and `:addresses` the verbs of the name and the kinds
      they serve, so the subject is turned into one of those kinds first,
      or a verb of another name was meant. The call reached the contract
      of the name, a verb without a body, which answers no call.

      ~(1 | count !| [/actualType (/addresses | has ::vec/count)] | eq [::number true])

      ~(
        :count ::verb~(:x ::number) | [1] | count 1
        !| [type /addresses]
        |  eq [::verb/VerbWithoutBodyError #[::map/count ::set/count ::string/count ::vec/count]]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()
